Independent ifs run all the time. elif stops at the first true branch.

Python allows one else per if. This alone makes your code invalid.
if l1.val < l2.val:
    ...
else:
    l2.val < l1.val   # this line does NOTHING
    ...
else:                # ❌ illegal
    ...

Because if / if means:
“Check this”
“Now check that too, even if the first already ran”

One condition only? → if
One of many conditions? → if / elif / else
Multiple independent checks? → multiple if
